- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid information leakage in class object padding across trust boundaries
WHAT: Passing a C++ class object (or pointer/reference to it) across a trust boundary can leak sensitive data via uninitialized padding bits inside the object.
WHY: Padding bits may contain indeterminate values from prior memory usage, exposing secrets (keys, passwords, internal state) to less-trusted code or remote parties.
HOW: Before sending or sharing a class object across a trust boundary, ensure all padding is deterministically initialized (for example, zero-initialize the whole storage, then assign fields) or serialize only explicit fields into a separate, fully initialized transfer structure.
EXAMPLE: Before: `MyMsg msg; msg.id = userId; send(&msg, sizeof(msg));` After: `MyMsg msg{}; msg.id = userId; send(&msg, sizeof(msg));` or serialize only `id` into a packed transfer buffer.

- Avoid information leakage when passing C++ class objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Passing raw C++ class objects (by value or raw memory copy) across a trust boundary can leak sensitive data via padding bits and unused bit-fields whose contents are implementation-defined.
WHY: Attackers receiving such objects may read uninitialized padding/bit-field data, exposing prior in-memory values (keys, pointers, secrets) and enabling information disclosure or further exploitation.
HOW: Do not transmit raw class layouts; instead, define explicit serialized representations (e.g., structs without padding, versioned wire formats) and copy only initialized fields into these representations before sending across trust boundaries.
EXAMPLE: Before: `send(sock, &obj, sizeof(obj), 0);` After: define a packed/serialized DTO with only needed fields, populate it from `obj`, then `send(sock, &dto, sizeof(dto), 0);`

- Avoid syntactically ambiguous C++ declarations (vexing parse)
WHAT: Ambiguous C++ declarations (for example, `Gadget g(Widget(i));`) can be parsed as function declarations instead of object constructions, so expected constructors are never called.
WHY: Skipped construction or initialization can leave objects uninitialized or logic unexecuted, leading to undefined behavior, logic flaws, or security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Avoid declaration forms that can be parsed as functions; prefer uniform initialization (`Gadget g{Widget{i}};`), factory functions, or clearly separate declarations from calls; enable compiler warnings that detect vexing parses and fix all occurrences.
EXAMPLE: Before (ambiguous): `Gadget g(Widget(i));`
After (unambiguous): `Gadget g{Widget{i}};`

- Avoid syntactically ambiguous C++ declarations
WHAT: Ambiguous C++ declarations (e.g., `Widget w();` inside a function) can be parsed as function declarations instead of variable definitions, so constructors or initialization code never execute as intended.
WHY: Missing initialization can silently skip security-critical setup (e.g., zeroization, capability checks, invariant enforcement), leading to undefined behavior or bypassed protections.
HOW: When declaring local objects, avoid declaration forms that can be parsed as functions: elide empty parentheses (`Widget w;`) or use brace initialization (`Widget w{};`) to force object construction.
EXAMPLE: Noncompliant: `void f() { Widget w(); }`
EXAMPLE: Compliant: `void f() { Widget w; Widget w2{}; }`

- Ensure sufficient storage for C++ strings and null terminators
WHAT: Risk of buffer overflow or missing null terminator when reading into fixed-size character arrays and constructing strings.
WHY: Insufficient space or incorrect termination can cause memory corruption, crashes, or information disclosure.
HOW: Allocate buffers large enough for expected data plus the null terminator, or construct std::string using the exact number of characters read (e.g., via gcount(), readsome(), or formatted input) instead of manually inserting a terminator.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `char buf[32]; in.read(buf, 32); buf[31] = '\0'; std::string s(buf);` After (safer): `char buf[32]; in.read(buf, sizeof(buf)); std::string s(buf, in.gcount());`

- Guarantee sufficient storage for strings and null terminators
WHAT: Copying or reading strings into a buffer that is too small, or that does not reserve space for the null terminator, can cause buffer overflows (for example, using `std::cin >> buf` where `buf` is a fixed-size `char` array).
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, cause crashes, and enable code execution vulnerabilities.
HOW: Always ensure the destination buffer is large enough for the maximum expected data plus the null terminator; when using input APIs, explicitly bound the number of characters read to `buffer_size - 1` and do not rely on stateful width settings that reset after use.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe, may overflow): `char buf[12]; std::cin >> buf;` After (safer): `char buf[12]; std::cin.width(sizeof(buf)); std::cin >> std::setw(sizeof(buf)) >> buf;`



